{"subjectId":"python","id":"exceptions-advanced","title":"Exception Hierarchy & Advanced Handling","group":"Errors, Modules & Files","order":46,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Beyond <code>try</code>/<code>except</code>: the full class tree, designing your own error hierarchy, chaining, notes, exception groups, warnings and the patterns (EAFP, retry) that production code relies on.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy-3\"></i>The class tree</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Built-in exception hierarchy (abridged)</figcaption><pre><code>BaseException\n├── SystemExit              ← sys.exit()\n├── KeyboardInterrupt       ← Ctrl-C\n├── GeneratorExit           ← generator.close()\n├── BaseExceptionGroup\n└── Exception               ← everything you normally catch\n    ├── ArithmeticError     → ZeroDivisionError, OverflowError\n    ├── LookupError         → KeyError, IndexError\n    ├── OSError             → FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, TimeoutError, ConnectionError…\n    ├── ValueError          → UnicodeDecodeError…\n    ├── TypeError, AttributeError, NameError, ImportError (→ ModuleNotFoundError)\n    ├── RuntimeError        → RecursionError, NotImplementedError\n    ├── StopIteration, StopAsyncIteration\n    ├── ExceptionGroup\n    └── Warning             → DeprecationWarning, UserWarning, SyntaxWarning…</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(issubclass(KeyboardInterrupt, Exception))       # False\nprint(issubclass(KeyboardInterrupt, BaseException))   # True\nprint(issubclass(KeyError, LookupError), issubclass(ZeroDivisionError, ArithmeticError))   # True True\nprint([c.__name__ for c in FileNotFoundError.__mro__])\n# ['FileNotFoundError', 'OSError', 'Exception', 'BaseException', 'object']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>except Exception</code> deliberately does <strong>not</strong> catch <code>SystemExit</code>, <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> or <code>GeneratorExit</code> — those mean “stop now”, and swallowing them makes a program impossible to quit. A bare <code>except:</code> catches <code>BaseException</code>, which is why it's banned in good code.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import sys\ntry:\n    sys.exit(2)                    # sys.exit just raises SystemExit\nexcept SystemExit as e:\n    print(\"exit code\", e.code)     # exit code 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>Designing a custom hierarchy</h3>\n<p>A library should raise its <strong>own</strong> base exception, with specific subclasses underneath. Callers can then catch everything from your library in one clause, or one precise failure.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class PaymentError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for every error this library raises.\"\"\"\n\nclass CardDeclined(PaymentError):\n    def __init__(self, reason):\n        super().__init__(f\"card declined ({reason})\")\n        self.reason = reason\n\nclass GatewayTimeout(PaymentError):\n    pass\n\ndef charge(amount):\n    if amount &lt; 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"amount must be &gt;= 0\")     # caller bug → a built-in error\n    if amount &gt; 1000:\n        raise CardDeclined(\"limit\")\n    return \"ok\"\n\ntry:\n    charge(5000)\nexcept PaymentError as e:                           # the whole family\n    print(type(e).__name__, \"|\", e, \"|\", e.reason)  # CardDeclined | card declined (limit) | limit</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Inherit from <code>Exception</code></h3><p>Never from <code>BaseException</code> — your errors should be catchable with <code>except Exception</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Name ends in <code>Error</code></h3><p><code>ConfigError</code>, <code>RateLimitError</code> — the PEP 8 convention.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Carry data</h3><p>Store useful fields (<code>self.status</code>, <code>self.retry_after</code>) instead of making callers parse the message.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-recycle\"></i>Reuse built-ins for caller bugs</h3><p>Wrong argument type → <code>TypeError</code>; bad value → <code>ValueError</code>. Custom classes are for <em>domain</em> failures.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-join\"></i>Catching several types &amp; re-raising</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def to_int(x):\n    try:\n        return int(x)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError) as e:     # a tuple catches any of them\n        return type(e).__name__\n\nprint(to_int(\"7\"), to_int(\"x\"), to_int(None))   # 7 ValueError TypeError\n\nlog = []\ndef load(key):\n    try:\n        return {\"a\": 1}[key]\n    except KeyError:\n        log.append(f\"missing {key}\")          # do something useful…\n        raise                                 # …then re-raise the SAME exception\n\ntry:\n    load(\"b\")\nexcept KeyError as e:\n    print(repr(e), log)                       # KeyError('b') ['missing b']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>except</code> clauses are tried top to bottom and the first match wins — put <strong>subclasses before base classes</strong>. <code>except OSError:</code> above <code>except FileNotFoundError:</code> makes the second one unreachable.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>try:\n    1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError as e:\n    saved = e\nprint(\"e\" in dir(), type(saved).__name__)    # False ZeroDivisionError</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The name bound by <code>except … as e</code> is <strong>deleted</strong> when the block ends (to break a reference cycle through the traceback). Assign it to another name if you need it later.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Chaining: <code>__context__</code> vs <code>__cause__</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def implicit():\n    try:\n        1 / 0\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        raise ValueError(\"bad ratio\")              # error while handling another\n\ndef explicit():\n    try:\n        1 / 0\n    except ZeroDivisionError as e:\n        raise ValueError(\"bad ratio\") from e       # \"this caused that\"\n\ndef hidden():\n    try:\n        1 / 0\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        raise ValueError(\"bad ratio\") from None    # hide the internal detail\n\ndef inspect(f):\n    try:\n        f()\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return type(e.__cause__).__name__, type(e.__context__).__name__, e.__suppress_context__\n\nprint(inspect(implicit))   # ('NoneType', 'ZeroDivisionError', False)\nprint(inspect(explicit))   # ('ZeroDivisionError', 'ZeroDivisionError', True)\nprint(inspect(hidden))     # ('NoneType', 'ZeroDivisionError', True)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Attribute set</th><th>Traceback says</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>raise New()</code> inside <code>except</code></td><td><code>__context__</code></td><td>“During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred”</td></tr><tr><td><code>raise New() from e</code></td><td><code>__cause__</code> (+ <code>__context__</code>)</td><td>“The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception”</td></tr><tr><td><code>raise New() from None</code></td><td><code>__suppress_context__ = True</code></td><td>Only the new exception</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-notes\"></i>Adding context: <code>add_note</code> (3.11+)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import traceback\n\ndef parse_row(n, text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        e.add_note(f\"while parsing row {n} of users.csv\")\n        raise\n\ntry:\n    parse_row(3, \"abc\")\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e.__notes__)                                       # ['while parsing row 3 of users.csv']\n    print(traceback.format_exception_only(e)[-1].strip())    # while parsing row 3 of users.csv</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notes are printed under the error message in tracebacks — a lightweight way to add context without wrapping the exception in a new type.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>ExceptionGroup and <code>except*</code> (3.11+)</h3>\n<p>When several independent operations fail at once (parallel tasks, validating many fields), raise them <strong>together</strong> as an <code>ExceptionGroup</code>. <code>except*</code> handles each type inside the group; several <code>except*</code> clauses can run for one group.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def validate(user):\n    errors = []\n    if not user.get(\"name\"):\n        errors.append(ValueError(\"name is required\"))\n    if not isinstance(user.get(\"age\"), int):\n        errors.append(TypeError(\"age must be an int\"))\n    if \"@\" not in user.get(\"email\", \"\"):\n        errors.append(ValueError(\"email is invalid\"))\n    if errors:\n        raise ExceptionGroup(\"invalid user\", errors)\n\ntry:\n    validate({\"name\": \"\", \"age\": \"x\", \"email\": \"nope\"})\nexcept* ValueError as eg:\n    print(\"values:\", [str(e) for e in eg.exceptions])   # values: ['name is required', 'email is invalid']\nexcept* TypeError as eg:\n    print(\"types:\", [str(e) for e in eg.exceptions])    # types: ['age must be an int']</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>eg = ExceptionGroup(\"batch\", [ValueError(1), KeyError(2), ValueError(3)])\nvalues, rest = eg.split(ValueError)\nprint(len(values.exceptions), [type(e).__name__ for e in rest.exceptions])   # 2 ['KeyError']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code> raises an <code>ExceptionGroup</code> when tasks fail, so <code>except*</code> is the natural way to handle concurrent failures.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-search\"></i>Inspecting tracebacks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import traceback\n\ndef inner():\n    return 1 / 0\ndef outer():\n    return inner()\n\ntry:\n    outer()\nexcept ZeroDivisionError as e:\n    frames = traceback.extract_tb(e.__traceback__)\n    print([f.name for f in frames])                          # ['&lt;module&gt;', 'outer', 'inner']\n    print(traceback.format_exception_only(e)[0].strip())     # ZeroDivisionError: division by zero</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>traceback.format_exc()</code> returns the full traceback as a string (for logging); <code>logger.exception(\"msg\")</code> does the same automatically inside an <code>except</code> block.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i><code>assert</code> — for invariants, not validation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def mean(xs):\n    assert xs, \"mean() of an empty list\"     # a developer's sanity check\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n\ntry:\n    mean([])\nexcept AssertionError as e:\n    print(e)                                 # mean() of an empty list</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>python -O</code> strips every <code>assert</code>. Never use it to check user input, permissions or anything that must hold in production — <code>raise ValueError</code> instead. And <code>assert (cond, \"msg\")</code> is <strong>always true</strong> (a non-empty tuple); Python emits a <code>SyntaxWarning</code> for it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-circle\"></i>Warnings</h3>\n<p>A warning reports something suspicious without stopping the program — most often a deprecation. Warnings are printed once per location by default and can be filtered or turned into errors.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import warnings\n\ndef old_api():\n    warnings.warn(\"old_api() is deprecated; use new_api()\", DeprecationWarning, stacklevel=2)\n    return 42\n\nwith warnings.catch_warnings(record=True) as caught:\n    warnings.simplefilter(\"always\")\n    result = old_api()\n\nprint(result, caught[0].category.__name__)   # 42 DeprecationWarning\nprint(caught[0].message)                     # old_api() is deprecated; use new_api()</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Filter action</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>\"default\"</code></td><td>Print the first occurrence per location</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"always\"</code></td><td>Print every occurrence</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"ignore\"</code></td><td>Never print</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"error\"</code></td><td>Turn the warning into an exception — great in test suites (<code>python -W error</code>, <code>pytest -W error</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i><code>finally</code> beats <code>return</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def f():\n    try:\n        return \"from try\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"finally runs first\")   # finally runs first\n\nprint(f())                            # from try</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import warnings\n\n# A return inside finally silently DISCARDS an in-flight exception.\n# Python 3.14 warns about it at compile time (PEP 765); silenced here to show the behaviour.\nsrc = \"\"\"\ndef swallow():\n    try:\n        raise ValueError(\"lost!\")\n    finally:\n        return \"finally wins\"\n\"\"\"\nwith warnings.catch_warnings():\n    warnings.simplefilter(\"ignore\", SyntaxWarning)\n    ns = {}\n    exec(src, ns)\nprint(ns[\"swallow\"]())      # finally wins</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never <code>return</code>, <code>break</code> or <code>continue</code> out of a <code>finally</code> block — the exception being propagated vanishes without a trace.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>EAFP, cost and the retry pattern</h3>\n<p>Setting up a <code>try</code> block is essentially free in Python 3.11+ (“zero-cost” exceptions); <em>raising</em> costs a few microseconds. So EAFP — just try and handle failure — is idiomatic when failure is the exception, not the rule.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def get_port(cfg):\n    try:\n        return int(cfg[\"port\"])\n    except (KeyError, ValueError):\n        return 8080\n\nprint(get_port({\"port\": \"5432\"}), get_port({}), get_port({\"port\": \"x\"}))   # 5432 8080 8080</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — retry with exponential backoff</figcaption><pre><code>import time\n\ndef retry(fn, attempts=3, base_delay=0.001, retry_on=(ConnectionError, TimeoutError)):\n    for attempt in range(1, attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except retry_on:\n            if attempt == attempts:\n                raise                                    # out of attempts: propagate\n            time.sleep(base_delay * 2 ** (attempt - 1))  # exponential backoff\n\ncalls = []\ndef flaky():\n    calls.append(1)\n    if len(calls) &lt; 3:\n        raise ConnectionError(\"connection reset\")\n    return \"connected\"\n\nprint(retry(flaky), len(calls))   # connected 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Retry only <em>transient</em> errors (timeouts, connection resets), cap the attempts, back off exponentially (add jitter in production) and log each failure.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Retrying everything — a <code>ValueError</code> from bad input will fail identically every time.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview questions</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why shouldn't you write except: or except BaseException:?</summary><div><p>They catch <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and <code>SystemExit</code> too, so Ctrl-C and <code>sys.exit()</code> stop working, and they hide real bugs. Catch <code>Exception</code> at most (e.g. at a top-level request handler that logs and continues), and specific types everywhere else.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between raise X from e and raise X from None?</summary><div><p><code>from e</code> records <code>e</code> as the explicit cause (<code>__cause__</code>) and the traceback shows both, linked by “direct cause”. <code>from None</code> suppresses the context so only the new exception is shown — useful when the original is an implementation detail.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does except* do?</summary><div><p>It matches exceptions <em>inside</em> an <code>ExceptionGroup</code>. Each <code>except*</code> clause receives a sub-group of the matching exceptions; multiple clauses can run for the same group, and any unmatched exceptions are re-raised in a new group.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you define a custom exception?</summary><div><p>When callers need to react to a specific domain failure (<code>CardDeclined</code>, <code>RateLimited</code>) differently from others. Give the library one base class so callers can catch all of its errors, and attach structured data as attributes.</p></div></details></section>"}